InclusiveCache: an opt-in to skip the outer Release for a clean victim (last-level caches only).

Tree: generators/rocket-chip-inclusive-cache (base 8e157c8).  Apply with
  git -C $CHIPYARD_DIR/generators/rocket-chip-inclusive-cache apply <this patch>

WHY.  In a full last-level cache every miss evicts.  A clean victim's Release reaches the
TLCacheCork, which answers it with a locally generated ReleaseAck on the same inner D channel
as the refill's GrantData: nine D messages per 64-byte block.  A cycle-accurate model of this
SoC's generated L2 + cork (iiswc-tutorial fpga/pynq-z2/rtl_study/l2tb) attributes the measured
DRAM plateau to that ReleaseAck and predicts that removing it takes a saturated miss stream
from 6.13 to 7.9997 B/cycle -- the 64-bit link -- with no latency stall under a concurrent
writer (unlike reordering the cork's arbiter, patches/0091, which the same model shows can
stall a Put for 6,640 cycles).

WHAT.  A CDE Field, InclusiveCacheSkipCleanRelease, default false.  When true, MSHR still
schedules the Directory invalidate for the victim exactly as before, but requests the C
channel only when the victim is dirty, and marks w_releaseack itself in the cycle that
schedule fires.  Scala-level `if`, so with the Field false the elaborated Chisel -- and the
Verilog -- are unchanged.

LEGALITY.  A Release informs a manager that tracks capabilities.  A last-level cache's outer
managers are all at most UNCACHED (InclusiveCacheParameters.lastLevel), so nothing downstream
tracks one; the outer node is a TLCacheCork, which ties off B and keeps no per-address state.
MSHR requires lastLevel when the Field is set, so the option cannot be switched on for a cache
whose outer side could track capabilities.

--- a/design/craft/inclusivecache/src/Parameters.scala
+++ b/design/craft/inclusivecache/src/Parameters.scala
@@ -27,6 +27,23 @@
 import freechips.rocketchip.util.property.cover
 import scala.math.{min,max}
 
+/** Skip the outer Release for a CLEAN victim.  Default false: the generated Verilog is unchanged.
+  *
+  * Every miss in a full cache evicts, and the eviction of a clean block sends a Release that a
+  * last-level cache's outer side answers with a ReleaseAck on the same D channel as the refill's
+  * GrantData -- nine D messages per 64-byte block where eight would do.  With this set, a clean
+  * victim is invalidated in the Directory exactly as before but no C-channel Release is issued;
+  * a dirty victim still sends ReleaseData.
+  *
+  * PRECONDITION, enforced by a require in MSHR: the cache is LAST-level, i.e. every outer
+  * manager is at most RegionType.UNCACHED, so nothing downstream can hold or track a cache
+  * capability that a Release would update.  In practice the outer node is a TLCacheCork, which
+  * ties off B, keeps no per-address permission state and generates the ReleaseAck locally.
+  * Do NOT enable it for a cache whose outer side is another capability-tracking manager.
+  *
+  * Measured and modelled in iiswc-tutorial fpga/pynq-z2/docs/MEMORY_BANDWIDTH.md section 6. */
+case object InclusiveCacheSkipCleanRelease extends Field[Boolean](false)
+
 case class CacheParameters(
   level:       Int,
   ways:        Int,
--- a/design/craft/inclusivecache/src/MSHR.scala
+++ b/design/craft/inclusivecache/src/MSHR.scala
@@ -94,6 +94,11 @@
     val nestedwb  = Flipped(new NestedWriteback(params))
   })
 
+  private val skipCleanRelease = params.p(InclusiveCacheSkipCleanRelease)
+  require(!skipCleanRelease || params.lastLevel,
+    "InclusiveCacheSkipCleanRelease needs a last-level cache: every outer manager at most UNCACHED, " +
+    "so no downstream manager tracks the capability a clean Release would give up")
+
   val request_valid = RegInit(false.B)
   val request = Reg(new FullRequest(params))
   val meta_valid = RegInit(false.B)
@@ -183,7 +188,13 @@
   val no_wait = w_rprobeacklast && w_releaseack && w_grantlast && w_pprobeacklast && w_grantack
